<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Suleiman the Magnificent</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1494">1494</a>–<a href="/years/1566">1566</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Kanuni · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-dynasty">Ottoman dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Suleiman the Magnificent, known in Turkish as Kanuni, the Lawgiver, was Ottoman sultan from 1520 to 1566. He conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a>, won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mohacs">Battle of Mohács</a>, besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> and extended Ottoman power in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> treat his reign as the height of Ottoman power and legal codification.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/suleiman-the-magnificent.jpg" alt="Suleiman the Magnificent" width="320" height="374" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Circle of Titian · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:EmperorSuleiman.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Suleiman succeeded his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/selim-i">Selim I</a> on 30 September 1520. His reign was the longest of any Ottoman sultan, and by its end the empire had at least 25 million subjects. He took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a> in 1521 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a> in 1522, and at Mohács in 1526 he broke the strength of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-hungary">Kingdom of Hungary</a>, much of which was then incorporated directly into the empire. His defeat at the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> in 1529 stopped his advance into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. In the east he fought the Shia Safavid Empire of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, entering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> in 1535.</p><p>At sea he appointed Khair ad Din, known to Europeans as Barbarossa, admiral-in-chief to rebuild the fleet. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden">Aden</a> in 1538, and although they failed against the Portuguese at the siege of Diu that year, they went on to take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>. In 1535 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> led a Holy League to victory over the Ottomans at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a>. This and the war against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> led Suleiman to accept an alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-i-of-france">Francis I of France</a> against Charles.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Subjects knew him as Kanuni, the Lawgiver, although the epithet is absent from 16th and 17th-century Ottoman sources and may date from the early 18th century. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharia">Sharia</a> lay outside the sultan's power to change, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanun">Kanuns</a>, covering criminal law, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/land-tenure">land tenure</a> and taxation, depended on his will. He collected the judgments of the nine sultans before him, removed duplications, chose between contradictory rulings and issued a single code, the kanun-i Osmani, with the help of his chief judicial official <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebussuud-efendi">Ebussuud Efendi</a>. It lasted more than three hundred years.</p><p>His patronage drew artists to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/topkapi-palace">Topkapı Palace</a>, where the Ehl-i Hiref, the Community of the Craftsmen, employed painters, bookbinders and goldsmiths. A payroll register of 1526 lists 40 societies with more than 600 members. The poets Fuzûlî and Bâki worked under him. In 1534 he broke with tradition by marrying Hürrem, a former concubine.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Suleiman died on 6 September 1566, aged 71, on campaign in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, before an Ottoman victory at the siege of Szigetvár. His Grand Vizier Sokollu Mehmed Pasha kept the death secret during the retreat so that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/selim-ii">Selim II</a> could be enthroned. His body was taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a>, while his heart and some organs were buried at Turbék, where a mausoleum became a pilgrimage site.</p><p>His image as an ideal ruler was built in his own reign, notably by the chancellor Celalzade Mustafa. Later Ottoman writers of advice literature held his reign up as a model and presented departures from it as decline. Western historians long accepted that account, but since the 1980s scholars have overwhelmingly rejected the idea of decline after his death, seeing instead a period of crisis and adaptation.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Suleiman the Magnificent” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Ottoman Sultan <b>Suleiman the Magnificent</b> backed Zápolya, besieged Vienna in 1529 and occupied Buda in 1541, leaving Hungary divided between a Habsburg ‘Royal Hungary’ in the north and west, an Ottoman province in the centre and an autonomous Principality of Transylvania in the east.</blockquote><a href="/empire/habsburg-monarchy">Habsburg Monarchy · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Black Army was disbanded in the 1490s the southern fortress line decayed, Belgrade fell in 1521, and on 29 August 1526 <b>Suleiman the Magnificent</b> destroyed the Hungarian army at the Battle of Mohács.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-hungary-1301-1526">Kingdom of Hungary (1301-1526) · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The reign of <b>Suleiman the Magnificent</b> (r. 1520–1566) produced its most ambitious campaigns: the capture of Belgrade in 1521, the destruction of the Hungarian royal army at the Battle of Mohács in 1526, the first siege of Vienna in 1529, and the naval operations of Hayreddin Barbarossa, whose…</blockquote><a href="/empire/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Later princes had less room: Petru Rareș lost his throne and the Tighina (Bender) region when <b>Suleiman the Magnificent</b> invaded in 1538, and Dimitrie Cantemir’s alliance with Peter the Great ended in the Russian defeat on the Pruth in 1711.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-moldavia">Principality of Moldavia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>And at Mohács in 1526 the army of <b>Suleiman the Magnificent</b> destroyed the army of the kingdom of Hungary, whose king died in the retreat.</blockquote><a href="/essays/the-military-revolution">The Military Revolution</a></li><li><blockquote>He governed through the sharia and through kanun, the dynastic law issued in his own name, and <b>Suleiman the Magnificent</b> was remembered as Kanuni, “the Lawgiver”.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ottoman-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Ottoman Empire</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trabzon</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. Died in Szigetvár, in what is now <a href="/countries/hungary">Hungary</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.01,39.72&amp;year=1540" aria-label="Trabzon on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5522 700 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Suleiman the Magnificent"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5972 970h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5972 970h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.01,39.72&amp;year=1540">What was here in 1540? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
